## Headlines from this cycle (2026-07-11 → 2026-07-13)

1. **Acemoglu + Autor join forces** on "Building Pro-Worker AI" (NBER w34854) — a normative reframe: current AI deployment favors substitution over augmentation due to incentives/ideology, not technological necessity. Autor's first distinct 2026 contribution.
2. **Two independent studies (Fed Notes + Stanford/SSRN) both find no aggregate job-posting or employment decline** from AI adoption, despite genAI usage hitting 35.9% of US workers — further erodes the "fast severe displacement" camp.
3. **Indeed Hiring Lab: AI-exposed jobs flipped from steepest declines to leading the recovery** — but 71% of that recovery is senior roles. Aggregate stability, real seniority-biased reallocation underneath.
4. **Gap-fills from last cycle:** PitchBook supplies the AI valuation premium (~4x at Series D+, 84% at Series A); Indeed supplies job-postings data.
5. **PIIE's Kolko** delivers a useful check on the whole field: the literature is genuinely contradictory and too immature (1-2 years of data) for confident causal claims either direction.
6. **"Distribution matters more than magnitude"** is now the best-evidenced thesis in the debate — three independent sources (Indeed, PwC, PitchBook) all point the same way this cycle.

One thing I couldn't complete: the agent-memory files (`coverage.md`, `indicators.md`, `debates.md`, `sources.md`) weren't refreshed this run due to budget exhaustion. Worth doing that update before the next cycle so dedup/continuity stays accurate — happy to do it now if you want to lift the budget cap, or first thing next time.
